6,0.030088143,0.0025088484,-0.015272559,-0.012260137,-0.002233762,0.013168072,-0.04059855,-0.01683589,-0.0010868169,0.004296159,0.0037519988,0.036149066,-0.013985815,-0.021285377,0.0378086,0.039203577,0.012626919,0.022018941,0.019806223,-0.005856485,0.0044284407,0.03285404,0.001587384,-0.0033311015,0.008929036,-0.0028485728,-0.0067704334,0.025061427,0.002702762,0.003343127,-0.0030079123,-0.030545117,0.02094866,0.007720459,-0.007846728,0.0067704334,0.0016099322,0.09976468,0.009079356,0.01109365,-0.005002665,0.007612228,-0.002247291,0.040887166,-0.00028917898,0.03023245,-0.025927274,-0.011394291,0.011063586,0.001579868,0.012963637,0.006391626,-0.0051409598,-0.021345505,0.017930225,0.012602868,-0.0006456264,-0.001113123,0.010191727,0.030761579,0.03059322,-0.0016264673,0.018759994,-0.009289805,-0.022006916,-0.017677685,-0.03097804,0.03177173,-0.0018790057,-0.00080346287,-0.016054224,-0.023257582,0.016631456,-0.03153122,-0.019649891,0.0063254847,-0.04018968,-0.008027112,-0.009939189,0.022908838,-0.025325991,0.019601788,-0.041849215,0.02549435,0.012590841,0.01584979,0.01164683,0.010955355,-0.031386912,-0.03528322,-0.016246635,0.027322248,-0.017160583,-0.0068967026,-0.008285664,-0.0043172035,-0.02304112,-0.009608484,0.0058324337,-0.002821515,0.010462304,0.001903057]'::vector, 'imposed by the graph structure, resources can only be transferred between connected nodes, which restricts the players’ strategies (i.e., action space) In this paper, we assume that each player has M units of resources The graph is represented by G = {V , E} , where V is the node set and E is the edge set The graph G contains N nodes, denoted as |V| = N The connectivity of graph   G   is described by the adjacency matrix   H , where   H ij   = 1   if   ( i, j )   ∈ E ,   H ij   = 0  otherwise The player resource distribution is represented by  d Each node is assigned a unique ID ranging from 0 to   N   − 1 , as shown in Figure 4 This ID will be used in Section III-A It is important to note that on-graph CBG is a multi- step game, while conventional CBG is a single-step game In conventional CBG, there is no graph constraint, allowing any desired resource allocation to be achieved in a single step, and the outcome is determined permanently However, in an on-graph CBG, the graph constraint means that a resource dis- tribution may require multiple steps to achieve or might not be achievable at all For example, if one wants to move resources from node   i   to node   j , but   H ij   = 0   and   H ik   =   H kj   = 1 , the transfer can only be completed in two time steps: first by moving resources from node   i   to node   k , and then from node   k   to node   j This highlights that on-graph CBG explicitly inherits the property of multi-step resource transition There- fore, as a multi-step game, on-graph CBG begins with initial resource distributions   d 1 0 ,   d 2 0   for both players Starting from the initial state, players adjust their resource allocations across connected nodes The current allocation affects the possible allocations in the next step This requires calculating the valid allocation space (referred to as the action space hereafter) at each game step A primary challenge in applying RL to on- graph CBG is the fixed structure of the DNN conflicting with the dynamically changing valid action space at each step The dimension of the DNN output is pre-defined in RL In DQN, the outputs are each action’s Q value given the current state, and in PPO, the outputs are the probability of taking each action given the current state However, the graph constraint results in a dynamically changing action space, which in turn necessitates a flexible output dimension To resolve this, we introduce a novel action-displacement adjacency matrix that selectively filters the DNN’s output, ensuring that only valid actions are considered at each game step Moreover,   since   the   distribution   at   the   next   step   only depends on the distribution at the current step, but not the distributions at previous steps, which indicates that the on- graph CBG satisfies the Markov property and thus can be modeled as an MDP B Reinforcement Learning for GRAG  Since both players execute actions simultaneously from distinct action spaces, which, due to the graph constraint, vary with the current resource distribution, the state tran- sition function   P   ( s t +1 | s ,   a )   for an individual player cannot be explicitly expressed Therefore, we adopt model-free rein- forcement learning approaches: the value-based method Deep'::text, 'doc'::varchar(50), NULL, '{}'::jsonb, ''+1':538 '0':87,106,221,292,295 '1':80,109,227,291 '2':294 '4':114 'achiev':164,196,201 'across':309 'action':20,334,364,387,406,420,438,453,512,516 'action-displac':437 'adjac':74,440 'adjust':305 'adopt':551 'affect':315 'alloc':161,308,314,318,328 'allow':157 'appli':345 'approach':558 'assign':100 'assum':26 'b':503 'base':562 'begin':285 'calcul':325 'cannot':545 'cbg':134,142,151,181,262,284,350,490 'challeng':343 'chang':362,419 'complet':233 'conflict':358 'connect':12,66,310 'consid':455 'constraint':156,184,414,523 'contain':58 'convent':141,150 'current':313,393,409,475,527 'd':96,290,293 'deep':564 'defin':378 'denot':61 'depend':469 'describ':71 'desir':159 'determin':173 'dimens':370,429 'dis':189 'displac':439 'distinct':515 'distribut':92,289,463,472,480,529 'dnn':357,373,446 'dqn':382 'due':519 'dynam':361,418 'e':42,50,84 'edg':53 'ensur':449 'exampl':205 'execut':511 'explicit':263,547 'express':548 'figur':113 'filter':444 'first':238 'fix':353 'flexibl':427 'forcement':556 'fore':274 'free':554 'function':534 'g':40,57,69 'game':139,148,280,339,458 'given':391,407 'grag':507 'graph':4,36,56,68,133,155,180,183,261,283,349,413,489,522 'h':76,78,85,219,223,225 'hereaft':336 'highlight':257 'howev':175,411 'i.e':19 'id':103,116 'iii':123 'iii-a':122 'ij':79,86,220 'ik':224 'import':127 'impos':1 'indic':485 'individu':543 'inherit':264 'initi':287,302 'introd