 adjacency matrix   H ′   ∈ { 0 ,   1 } N   × N   as:  H ′   =  N   − 1 X  i =0  diag( e i )   ·   H   ·   P i (5) This definition ensures that the   i -th row of   H   is cyclically left-shifted by   i   positions, and the result placed back into the  i -th row of   H ′ Let   d   =   ( d 0 , , d N   − 1 )   ∈   I ( N   ) N   be the node-level resource distribution with   P  i   d i   =   M   total resources We define a   lifting operator   Φ :   I ( N   ) N   → { 0 ,   1 } M   × N   which expands   d   into   a   row-wise   stack   of   one-hot   vectors In particular, if resource   j   is located at node   n j   , then the   j -th row of   Φ( d )   equals the canonical basis vector   e n j   ∈   R N With a lifting operator, we have a matrix form resource distribution  ˆ d   = Φ( d ) Based on that, we define the valid action matrix as:  J   :=  ˆ d   ·   H ′ (6) To isolate the valid actions for resource   j , we compute the  j -th row of   J   as:  J j, ·   =   e ⊤  n j   ·   H ′  =  N   − 1 X  i =0  e ⊤  n j   ·   diag( e i )   ·   H   ·   P i  =   e ⊤  n j   ·   diag( e n j   )   ·   H   ·   P n j  =   H n j   , ·   ·   P n j Here, the final expression represents the   n j   -th row of   H   left- shifted by   n j   positions Therefore, the   k -th element of this row equals:  J j,k   =   H n j   , ( n j   + k ) mod   N (7) Thus,   J j,k   = 1   if and only if the original adjacency matrix  H   allows a transition from node   n j   to node   ( n j   +   k ) mod   N This completes the proof Since all the proof processes are reversible, conversely, all valid actions   k   satisfy   J jk   = 1   for resource   j In Figure 2,'::text, 'doc'::varchar(50), NULL, '{}'::jsonb, ''+1':105 '0':24,32,64,76,94,136,146,187,218,322 '1':11,26,34,65,77,87,91,137,143,190,219,319,392,435 '2':441 '4':96 '5':153 '6':294 '7':387 'accord':53 'action':15,287,299,430 'adjac':61,133,399 'allow':402 'augment':132 'back':176 'base':280 'basi':258 'canon':257 'complet':417 'comput':304 'const