837,-0.0077994633,0.008613376,0.0059761708,-0.01320205,0.010632136,0.030685153,-0.0413237,-0.00445409,0.0070880908,0.009459333,0.015675832,-0.025660682,0.026275925,-0.056858536,0.0047969585,0.042989977,0.009177348,0.0058223605,0.015150313,0.006347879,0.033658817,-0.0029720634,0.012817524,0.013868561,-0.010036121,-0.02325099,-0.0027461546,0.060088553,0.0018056687,0.023084361,0.024930084,-0.0066971565,0.020482404,-0.0056461194,-0.004742484,0.004313097,0.026224654,0.0543463,0.013881379,-0.030454438,-0.009497785,0.017111395,0.045886736,-0.007004777,0.0020251689,0.019072477,-0.0073316237,-0.010491144,0.016944766,-0.00080630236,0.011343509,0.018316243,0.007536704,-0.05162899,0.006014623,-0.008132719,-0.003499184,-0.004463703,0.025494056,0.023827778,0.011414005,-0.012279188,0.01884176,0.035171285,0.006754835,0.0009124475,-0.027634582,-0.009459333,-0.0060370537,-0.0015773566,-0.03758098,-0.025276158,-0.0269168,-0.046604518,-0.004845024,0.03340247,0.0053032506,-0.0002178979,0.012670123,0.019597994,-0.016367977,0.014945233,-0.02350734,0.045502212,0.0031483043,0.007011186,0.025006989,-0.024455836,-0.013778838,-0.025647866,0.002608366,0.026993705,-0.027121881,0.034043346,-0.022046141,0.03189,-0.03768352,-0.012112561,0.01677814,0.009061989,0.003960615,0.0074277553]'::vector, 'The conversation details a comprehensive research effort to improve Large Language Models'' (LLMs) graph reasoning abilities through reinforcement learning (RL), culminating in the development of the G1 approach. The authors recognize that, despite LLMs'' general progress, they struggle with graph-structured data—a crucial aspect for general-purpose intelligence. Previous methods, such as supervised fine-tuning and pretraining on graph-specific tasks, are hindered by the scarcity and heterogeneity of graph data.

To address these challenges, the authors introduce Erdős, the largest and most diverse graph-theoretic dataset to date, comprising 50 tasks of varying complexity (from simple properties to NP-hard problems), 100,000 training, and 5,000 test instances, all derived from real-world graphs. Erdős enables rule-based reward assignment, eliminating reliance on human annotation and facilitating scalable RL training.

The G1 approach leverages RL—specifically, Group Relative Policy Optimization—using synthetic graph-theoretic tasks to elicit and enhance latent graph reasoning skills in pretrained LLMs. The pipeline includes an optional supervised fine-tuning (SFT) warm-up phase (either direct answer or chain-of-thought-based) to alleviate cold-start issues, particularly for challenging tasks where initial model accuracy is low.

Empirical results demonstrate that G1-trained models (notably G1-7B and G1-3B) achieve substantial improvements over both proprietary and open-source baselines on the Erdős benchmark, with G1-7B attaining 66.16% average accuracy (compared to 47.16% for the much larger Qwen2.5-72B-Instruct). Direct-SFT alone provides strong baselines, but RL-trained models show superior scaling and generalization.

G1 models exhibit robust zero-shot transfer to unseen graph tasks, domains, and data encodings, outperforming models trained on alternative benchmarks (GraphWiz, GraphArena) and excelling in real-world applications such as node classification and link prediction on citation networks (Cora, PubMed). Notably, RL training on graph tasks does not degrade, and sometimes even enhances, general reasoning abilities on unrelated benchmarks (GSM8K, MATH, MMLU-pro).

Analysis reveals that G1-RL training encourages models to adopt more effective, model-aware graph reasoning strategies (favoring BFS and intuitive search over more complex algorithms like Dijkstra’s, which base LLMs struggle to execute reliably). The authors further explore the impact of reward weighting and data mixture, showing that targeting harder tasks or applying soft reward scaling can adjust performance trade-offs across difficulty levels and foster transferable reasoning skills.

The work positions reinforcement learning on synthetic, verifiable graph-theoretic tasks as a scalable, data-efficient paradigm for building general-purpose LLM-based graph reasoners, moving beyond the limitations of previous methods. The models, data, and code are open-sourced to promote further research and accessibility.'::text, 'summary'::varchar(50), NULL, '{}'::jsonb, ''000':111,115 '100':110 '3b':223 '47.16':249 '5':114 '50':97 '66.16':244 '7b':219,242 'abil':16,330 'access':463 'accuraci':205,246 'achiev':224 'across':405 'address':78 'adjust':400 'adopt':349 'algorithm':366 'allevi':193 'alon':258 'altern':292 'analysi':339 'annot':136 'answer':185 'appli':395 'applic':302 'approach':28,144 'aspect':46 'assign':131 'attain':243 'author':30,82,378 'averag':245 'awar':354 'base':129,191,371,439 'baselin':234,261 'benchmark':238,293,333 'beyond':443 'bfs':359 'build':433 'chain':188 'chain-of-thought-bas':187 'challeng':80,200 'citat':311 'classif':306 'code':453 'cold':195 'cold-start':194 'compar':247 'complex':101,365 'comprehens':5 'compris':96 'convers':2 'cora':313 'crucial':45 'culmin':21 'data':43,76,286,387,429,451 'data-effici':428 'dataset':93 'date':95 'degrad':323 'demonstr':210 'deriv':119 'despit':33 'detail':3 'develop':24 'difficulti':406 'dijkstra':368 'direct':184,256 'direct-sft':255 'divers':89 'domain':284 'effect':351 'effici':430 'effort':7 'either':183 'elicit':159 'elimin':132 'empir':208 'enabl':126 'encod':287 'encourag':346 'enhanc':161,327 'erdő':84,125,237 'even':326 'excel':297 'execut':375 'exhibit':274 'explor':380 'facilit':138 'favor':358 'fine':58,176 'fine-tun':57,175 'foster':409 'g1':27,143,213,218,222,241,272,343 'g1-3b':221 'g1-7b':217,240 'g1-rl':342 'g1-trained':212 'general':35,49,271,328,435 'general-purpos':48,434 'graph':14,41,64,75,91,124,155,163,282,319,355,422,440 'graph-specif':63 'graph-structur':40 'graph-theoret':90,154,421 'grapharena':295 'graphwiz':294 'group':148 'gsm8k':334 'hard':108 'harder':392 'he