G1   charts a data-efficient and scalable course for developing LLMs with strong graph reasoning By demonstrating that RL can unlock latent graph understanding within general-purpose LLMs using synthetic data, our work suggests a possible paradigm shift away from reliance on heterogeneous real-world graphs to build graph foundation models This paves the way for more versatile AI systems capable of sophisticated reasoning across diverse data modalities 2   Related Work  Graph Reasoning Graph reasoning problems fall into two categories: domain-specific, which require understanding both graph structures and node/link attributes,   e.g , node classification, link prediction, and knowledge-based QA [ 15 ,   55   ,   17   ]; and domain-agnostic, also called   graph-theoretic problems , which focus solely on structural reasoning but find a lot of practical uses in various domains,  e.g , shortest paths, Hamiltonian paths, graph isomorphism [49, 33] For the latter problems that we study in this paper, people have studied the use of RL [ 27   ,   39   ] or unsupervised learning [ 18   ], often in conjunction with Graph Neural Networks (GNNs) [ 19   ,   48   ] that align with the solution structure [ 50   ] Yet these models are often built to solve each problem alone Recently, Sanford et al [32]   prove and validate the priority of the transformer models compared to GNNs on complex graph reasoning tasks requiring long-range dependencies In this work, we focus on building general-purpose graph reasoners that could solve a range of graph-theoretic problems by exploiting the strength of LLM pretraining, and find that the ability also generalizes to the former domain-specific graph tasks Benchmarking LLMs on Graph Reasoning There is a growing interest in evaluating LLMs’ graph reasoning abilities NLGraph [   40   ] evaluate LLMs on graph-theoretic tasks and discover preliminary yet brittle reasoning abilities in the face of spurious correlations and large graphs Later, GraphArena [ 36   ] and GraCoRe [ 54   ] include a broader task coverage and recently released LLMs, finding that even OpenAI o1-mini struggles a lot with complex tasks Moreover, GraphEval2000 [   45   ] and ProGraph [   22   ] emphasize code-oriented problem solving using library-based prompts, and GraphOmni [   47   ] unify varying graph types, encodings, and prompt styles for a comprehensive evaluation Overall, these benchmarks suggest that LLMs overall demonstrate moderate success on simple tasks but struggle with abstraction, generalization, and larger or more complex graph instances Nevertheless, these datasets are either too small (e.g., thousands of examples) or not diverse enough (e.g., 8 tasks in NLGraph) for training general-purpose graph reasoners, which motivates the design of   Erdős Improving LLMs on Graph Reasoning A major concern when using LLMs for graph tasks is the mismatch of data structure: LLMs take text sequences as input, while graphs have no natural order Fatemi et al [12]   analyzed different graph encoding schemes for LLMs, such as adjacency lists and real-name networks, revealing that no single strategy proved universally optimal across all tasks and models Subsequent explorations with different linearization orders [   6 ], graph embeddings [   29 ], or input modalities [   10   ] have generally resulted in only modest improvements Another thread of research proposes post-training LLMs using instruction tuning [ 25 ,   52   ] or preference tuning [   4 ,   41   ,   38 ] on curated datasets of graph problems However, the creation of diverse, high-quality instruction datasets at scale is challenging and expensive and requires extra supervision Furthermore, models trained via distillation may only learn to memorize patterns and overfit to graph tasks [   5 ]; in Section 5.2, we show that previous instruction-tuned models exhibit dramatic failures when generalizing to other data formats and reasoning tasks, while our RL training yields consistently better performance Reinforcement Learning for LLMs Reasoning'::text, 'doc'::varchar(50), NULL, '{}'::jsonb, ''10':506 '12':463 '15':109 '17':111 '18':169 '19':178 '2':1,75 '22':343 '25':526 '27':164 '29':502 '3':3 '32':202 '33':146 '36':312 '38':533 '39':165 '4':531 '40':286 '41':532 '45':340 '47':357 '48':179 '49':145 '5':576 '5.2':579 '50':186 '52':527 '54':315 '55':110 '6':499 '8':411 'abil':258,284,300 'abstract':386 'across':71,488 'adjac':473 'agnost':115 'ai':65 'al':201,462 'align':181 'alon':197 'also':116,259 'analyz':464 'anoth':514 'attribut':98 'away':44 'base':107,353 'benchmark':269,372 'better':606 'brittl':298 'broader':318 'build':54,231 'built':192 'call':117 'capabl':67 'categori':86 'challeng':553 'chart':5 'classif':101 'code':346 'code-ori':345 'compar':212 'complex':216,336,392 'comprehens':368 'concern':435 'conjunct':172 'consist':605 'correl':306 'could':238 'cours':12 'coverag':320 'creation':542 'curat':535 'data':8,36,73,446,595 'data-effici':7 'dataset':397,536,549 'demonstr':21,377 'depend':224 'design':425 'develop':14 'differ':465,496 'discov':295 'distil':564 'divers':72,408,544 'domain':88,114,137,265 'domain-agnost':113 'domain-specif':87,264 'dramat':589 'e.g':99,138,402,410 'effici':9 'either':399 'embed':501 'emphas':344 'encod':362,467 'enough':409 'erdő':427 'et':200,461 'evalu':280,287,369 'even':327 'exampl':405 'exhibit':588 'expens':555 'exploit':248 'explor':494 'extra':558 'face':303 'failur':590 'fall':83 'fatemi':460 'find':129,255,325 'focus':123,229 'format':596 'former':263 'foundat':56 'furthermor':560 'g1':4 'general':31,233,260,387,418,508,592 'general-purpos':30,232,417 'gnns':177,214 'gracor':314 'graph':18,27,52,55,78,80,94,119,143,174,217,235,244,267,272,282,291,309,360,393,420,431,440,455,466,500,538,574 'graph-theoret':118,243,290 'grapharena':311 'grapheval2000':339 'graphomni':356 'grow':277 'hamiltonian':141 'heterogen':48 'high':546 'high-qual':545 'howev':540 'improv':428,513 'includ':316 'input':453,